'' =============================================================================
''  test-VO-J-005 -- does the streamer capture DIGITAL PIN STATE into hub RAM,
''                   and does capturing a SMART pin give the flag while capturing
''                   its NEIGHBOUR gives the waveform?
'' =============================================================================
''  WHY THIS RUN EXISTS
''    Every streamer finding in our ledger is an OUTPUT result or an ADC result.
''    EF-054/055/059/062/063/064/065 -- not one of them has ever put the
''    Pins-to-WRFAST digital capture path on silicon.  The encoding hazards we
''    hold transfer to it (they are properties of the command word), but the
''    capture path itself is documentary only.
''
''    And the fact that decides how you USE it is documentary only too:
''
''      Silicon Doc v35 : "Initially, and on each subsequent NCO rollover,
''                         1/2/4/8/16/32 pins are read from {INB, INA}"
''      Silicon Doc v35 : "When a mode-related event occurs in a smart pin, it
''                         raises its IN signal to alert the cog(s)..."
''      Silicon Doc v35 : "The resultant 'A' will drive the IN signal in
''                         non-smart-pin modes."
''
''    Put together: capture reads IN, and on a pin RUNNING a smart-pin mode IN is
''    the smart pin's handshake, not the wire.  So streaming INA of a live SPI bus
''    records ready-pulses, not traffic.  Parallax states the same thing in one
''    clause in the Spin2 v55 LOGIC section -- "the streamer will record the smart
''    pin's IN signal and its output state, as read from an adjacent pin" -- and
''    ships a worked example built on it.  Nobody here has measured it.
''
''    This run measures it, with BOTH signals in the SAME buffer at the SAME
''    instant, so the contrast is one result rather than two runs compared.
''
''  Build:  pnut-ts -d test-vo-j-005-streamer-pin-capture.spin2
''          The -d is REQUIRED, or every line below prints nothing.
''
''  BENCH SETUP: BARE BOARD.  No jumper.  No external instrument.
''    The stimulus is generated on-chip by a smart pin and observed on-chip
''    through the %AAAA input selector, so nothing has to be wired.
''    Pins touched: P8..P15 and NOTHING else -- clear of P0..P7 (where the earlier
''    campaign rigs put their jumper), clear of P58..P63 (boot + DEBUG_PIN_TX/RX).
''    P8..P15 are certified electrically free, WEAKLY, before anything is driven
''    hard.  If something is attached, the program names the pins and stops.
''
''  PIN MAP -- the whole experiment is in this table
''    P8  BUS    smart pin, TRANSITION output mode.  It generates a square wave AND
''               it is the pin whose IN is the smart pin's completion flag.
''    P9  MON_R  smart pin mode OFF, A input selected from relative -1 (P_MINUS1_A).
''               Its IN therefore carries P8's PIN STATE.  Unfiltered.
''    P10 MON_F  smart pin mode OFF, A input from relative -2 (P_MINUS2_A), with
''               filt0 selected (P_FILT0_AB).  Same signal, filtered path.
''    P11 NULL   plain pin, no routing, driven low.  It rides along INSIDE the same
''               capture and MUST read all zeros.  If it does not, the lane mapping
''               is wrong and no other lane in that buffer can be believed.
''    P12..P15   used only by arm B, to find out where an unaligned base lands.
''
''  PREDICTIONS ON RECORD, WITH THEIR FALSIFYING OUTCOMES NAMED
''    A  (control) a 4-pin capture of statically driven pins returns those levels,
''       and returns DIFFERENT bytes for different levels.
''       Falsified if A1 == A2, or if any buffer comes back still full of sentinel.
''       IF A FAILS, NOTHING BELOW IS INTERPRETABLE and the program says so.
''    B  a 4-pin capture at base 12 reads P12..P15, not P8..P11.
''       Falsified by reading P8..P11 -- which would mean D[19:17] is a DENSE slot
''       selector rather than pin&7, and P2KB finding F-361 is backwards.
''    C  (no streamer at all) over a 20 ms window the cog sees P9 CHANGE about 40
''       times and P8 change ONCE.  Same contrast, no streamer involved, so it
''       isolates the IN rule from the capture path -- the simplest layer answers
''       first.  Count CHANGES, not levels: both signals sit high for about half
''       the window, so level counts cannot tell a flag from a square wave.
''       Falsified if P8 changes as often as P9 -- IN is then NOT repurposed, and
''       the KB block this test was written to ground (smart_pins.yaml
''       in_signal_semantics) is wrong.
''    D  THE HEADLINE.  In ONE capture: the MON_R lane shows the square wave
''       (~10 edges), the MON_F lane shows the same wave, the BUS lane shows at
''       most ONE edge -- the flag rising when the burst completes mid-window --
''       and the NULL lane shows none.
''       Falsified if the BUS lane matches MON_R: IN is not repurposed.
''       Falsified differently if BUS and MON_R are BOTH flat: the stimulus never
''       ran; arm C's result decides which.
''       The burst is deliberately armed to END INSIDE the window so the BUS lane
''       carries exactly one edge.  A lane with one edge is a LIVE lane carrying a
''       different signal -- not a dead lane.  That distinction is the whole reason
''       the burst is not left running.
''    E  edge spacing scales with the NCO ratio, exactly, until it does not.
''       The fastest ratio where measured spacing still equals the predicted
''       spacing IS the sustained ceiling, measured rather than asserted.
''       No ceiling is predicted here.  I could not establish one from any source
''       and will not invent one.
''
''  MEASURING COG == REPORTING COG, deliberately.
''    EF-057 is real: -d puts the highest-priority interrupt in the streamer's cog.
''    DEBUG_COGS below restricts it to cog 0, and cog 0 is where this measures.
''    VO-J-004 ran exactly this arrangement against a launched debug-free cog and
''    every digital count matched (EF-064), which is why a second cog is not built
''    here.  If any arm below returns jittery or irreproducible counts, that
''    judgement is what to revisit first -- move the measurement to a launched cog.
''
''  NOTHING GATES ACROSS ARMS except arm A, which gates by design: it is the only
''  arm that establishes the lane mapping every other arm reads through.
'' =============================================================================

CON
  _xtlfreq   = 20_000_000                ' P2 Eval/Edge default
  _clkfreq   = 200_000_000               ' 5 ns per clock

  '' EF-057: keep the debug interrupt out of every cog but the reporting one.
  DEBUG_COGS = %0000_0001

  BUS        = 8                         ' smart pin: TRANSITION output
  MON_R      = 9                         ' BUS + 1 -> selects A from relative -1
  MON_F      = 10                        ' BUS + 2 -> selects A from relative -2
  NULLPIN    = 11                        ' rides in the capture, must read 0

  BASE       = 8                         ' capture base: multiple of 4, as a 4-pin
                                         ' mode requires (D[18:17] are fixed zeros)
  ALT_BASE   = 12                        ' arm B: the next legal 4-pin base up

  GUARD_LO   = 8                         ' pins this program will touch: P8..P15
  GUARD_N    = 8

  '' Buffer.  A 4-pin mode packs TWO samples per byte, so 512 bytes is 1024
  '' sample sets.  Declared as longs so the buffer is long-aligned.
  BUF_LONGS  = 128
  BUF_BYTES  = BUF_LONGS * 4
  SAMPLES    = BUF_BYTES * 2

  SENTINEL   = $A5                       ' not $00, not $FF, not $55, not $AA,
                                         ' and not any nibble pattern this rig
                                         ' can legitimately produce ($11/$88/
                                         ' $33/$CC).  A buffer still holding it
                                         ' means the streamer never wrote.

  '' Streamer command words.  Both verified from the compiler listing before the
  '' run, which is how EF-065's vacuous first draft was caught:
  ''   X_4P_4DAC1_WFBYTE | X_WRITE_ON | (8<<17)  = $E090_0000
  ''   X_4P_4DAC1_WFBYTE | X_WRITE_ON | (12<<17) = $E098_0000
  '' Composed with `|`, never `+`.  Both bases are multiples of 4; an unaligned
  '' base in this mode does not round and does not error -- it changes the mode
  '' (EF-065) or vanishes (EF-065's `|` half).
  CAP_BASE   = X_4P_4DAC1_WFBYTE | X_WRITE_ON | (BASE     << 17) | SAMPLES
  CAP_ALT    = X_4P_4DAC1_WFBYTE | X_WRITE_ON | (ALT_BASE << 17) | SAMPLES

  '' NCO.  frequency = $8000_0000 * (sample_rate / clkfreq).
  NCO_1_1    = $8000_0000                ' one sample every clock
  NCO_1_2    = $4000_0000
  NCO_1_4    = $2000_0000
  NCO_1_8    = $1000_0000
  NCO_1_16   = $0800_0000

  '' TRANSITION output: X[15:0] is the base period in clocks; each transition
  '' happens one base period after the last; IN rises when Y transitions are done.
  '' X IS SIXTEEN BITS. "X[15:0] establishes a base period in clock cycles" --
  '' anything above 65,535 is silently truncated, which would move arm C's half
  '' period without saying so. Every period below is inside that range.
  FAST_PER   = 64                        ' clocks per half-cycle for arms D and E
  SLOW_PER   = 60_000                    ' 300 us per half-cycle, for arm C
  BURST_D    = 10                        ' 10 * 64 = 640 clocks: ENDS INSIDE the
                                         ' 1024-clock arm-D window, on purpose
  BURST_E    = 600                       ' 600 * 64 = 38,400 clocks: outlasts even
                                         ' the slowest arm-E window (16,384)
  BURST_C    = 40                        ' 40 * 300 us = 12 ms, inside arm C's
                                         ' 20 ms observation window
  C_SAMPLES  = 400                       ' arm C: 400 reads, 50 us apart = 20 ms.
                                         ' 50 us against a 300 us half period is
                                         ' six samples per half cycle -- clear of
                                         ' the aliasing that a 100 us interval
                                         ' would have flirted with
  C_INTERVAL = 50                        ' microseconds between arm C reads

  '' Expected whole-byte values for arm A.  A 4-pin sample is {P11,P10,P9,P8};
  '' bit 0 of the sample is the BASE pin.  Two samples per byte, and the Silicon
  '' Doc says shifted modes use "bits bottom-first, by default" (X_ALT_OFF), so
  '' the earlier sample occupies the LOW nibble.  With a STATIC pattern both
  '' nibbles are identical, which is why arm A cannot test that ordering claim --
  '' see the note in report_A.
  BYTE_NONE  = $00                       ' nibble %0000
  BYTE_P8    = $11                       ' nibble %0001 -- only the base pin high
  BYTE_P11   = $88                       ' nibble %1000 -- only the top pin high
  BYTE_LOW2  = $33                       ' nibble %0011 -- P8,P9 high
  BYTE_TOP2  = $CC                       ' nibble %1100 -- P14,P15 high (arm B)

  CAP_BUDGET = 2_000_000                 ' 10 ms ceiling on any one capture; a
                                         ' streamer that never starts must not
                                         ' hang the session (round-1 lesson:
                                         ' WAITXFI unbounded cost a bench slot)

VAR
  long bufl[BUF_LONGS]                   ' the capture buffer, long-aligned


PUB main() | ok

  debug("=== VO-J-005: streamer digital pin capture, and the IN contrast ===")
  debug("    clk=200MHz  base=P8  4-pin capture  1024 samples  512 bytes")

  if not certify_pins_free()
    debug("STOP -- P8..P15 are not electrically free. Nothing was driven hard.")
    terminator()
    return

  ok := arm_A_calibrate()
  if not ok
    debug("STOP -- arm A is the control for every lane reading below.")
    debug("   Arms B, D and E read the buffer through the mapping A establishes.")
    debug("   Running them now would produce numbers with no meaning. Stopping.")
    terminator()
    return

  arm_B_unaligned_base()
  arm_C_cog_level_contrast()
  arm_D_streamer_contrast()
  arm_E_rate_ladder()

  release_all()
  terminator()


PRI terminator()
  debug("@@@ TEST COMPLETE: VO-J-005 streamer pin capture @@@")
  debug("@@@ nothing further will print - safe to stop the session @@@")
  repeat


'' ============================================================================
''  Pin certification -- weakly, before anything is driven hard
'' ============================================================================
''  P8..P15 are held at a 15 kOhm LOW with DIR high.  An undriven pin then reads
''  0 for a stated reason instead of floating at whatever charge it kept, and
''  anything real attached to those pins overpowers 15 kOhm and reads 1.  That is
''  how a board with something on P8..P15 is detected BEFORE this program drives
''  those pins hard.  Same apparatus as EF-064's canvas.
PRI certify_pins_free() : ok | held
  pinclear(GUARD_LO addpins GUARD_N-1)
  wrpin(GUARD_LO addpins GUARD_N-1, P_LOW_15K)
  pinlow(GUARD_LO addpins GUARD_N-1)     ' DIR high, OUT low -> 15k to GIO
  waitms(1)
  held := pinread(GUARD_LO addpins GUARD_N-1)
  pinclear(GUARD_LO addpins GUARD_N-1)
  if held <> 0
    debug("   pins held by something external, mask(bit0=P8) = ", uhex_byte_(held))
    return false
  return true


'' ============================================================================
''  ARM A -- the control, and the lane calibration everything else reads through
'' ============================================================================
''  No smart pins.  No input routing.  Four pins driven hard to known levels and
''  captured.  This arm answers three separate questions at once:
''    1. does the Pins-to-WRFAST path write hub RAM at all?      (sentinel)
''    2. does it read the pins rather than returning a constant? (A1 vs A2)
''    3. which bit of a captured sample is which pin?            (A2 vs A3)
''  A rig that cannot fail proves nothing: A2 and A3 drive OPPOSITE ENDS of the
''  captured block, so a capture that always returns the same thing fails one of
''  them by construction.
PRI arm_A_calibrate() : ok | b1, b2, b3, u1, u2, u3, wrote1, wrote2, wrote3, late1, late2, late3

  debug("-- Arm A: capture-path control and lane calibration --")

  drive_static(0)                                  ' P8..P11 all low
  wrote1, late1 := capture(CAP_BASE, NCO_1_4, 0)
  b1, u1 := uniform_byte()

  drive_static(%0001)                              ' P8 high only
  wrote2, late2 := capture(CAP_BASE, NCO_1_4, 0)
  b2, u2 := uniform_byte()

  drive_static(%1000)                              ' P11 high only
  wrote3, late3 := capture(CAP_BASE, NCO_1_4, 0)
  b3, u3 := uniform_byte()

  release_all()

  if late1 or late2 or late3
    debug("A NOTE -- a capture hit its 10 ms budget without the streamer ever")
    debug("   signalling XFI. Bytes may still have landed; read the counts, but")
    debug("   treat any width or edge number below as suspect.")
  debug("   each row: bytes written, first byte, bytes unlike it, byte wanted")
  debug("A1 all low  : ", udec_(wrote1), " ", uhex_byte_(b1), " ", udec_(u1), " want ", uhex_byte_(BYTE_NONE))
  debug("A2 P8 high  : ", udec_(wrote2), " ", uhex_byte_(b2), " ", udec_(u2), " want ", uhex_byte_(BYTE_P8))
  debug("A3 P11 high : ", udec_(wrote3), " ", uhex_byte_(b3), " ", udec_(u3), " want ", uhex_byte_(BYTE_P11))

  if u1 <> 0 or u2 <> 0 or u3 <> 0
    debug("A FAILED -- a capture of STATIC pin levels came back non-uniform. The")
    debug("   pins did not move, so the buffer must not either. Something is")
    debug("   corrupting the capture; read the raw bytes before going further.")
    dump_head(string("A3"))
    return false

  if wrote1 == 0 or wrote2 == 0 or wrote3 == 0
    debug("A FAILED -- a buffer came back still full of sentinel. The streamer")
    debug("   never wrote hub RAM. This is NOT a lane-mapping problem: the")
    debug("   capture path did not run. Check WRFAST, the command word, and that")
    debug("   the inline PASM really executed from cog RAM.")
    return false

  if b1 == b2
    debug("A FAILED -- A1 and A2 returned the SAME byte with different pin levels.")
    debug("   The capture is not reading the pins. Everything else is void.")
    return false

  if b2 == BYTE_P8 and b3 == BYTE_P11
    debug("A PASSED -- path writes, discriminates, and lane N = pin BASE+N.")
    return true

  if b2 == BYTE_P11 and b3 == BYTE_P8
    debug("A FAILED (mapping) -- the lanes are REVERSED: the top pin landed in")
    debug("   bit 0. Not a bench fault -- our reading of the sample bit order is")
    debug("   backwards. Record it, flip the mapping, re-run. Do not reinterpret")
    debug("   arms B/D/E by hand.")
    return false

  debug("A FAILED -- unexpected bytes. Do not force a story; record them.")
  dump_head(string("A3"))
  return false


'' ============================================================================
''  ARM B -- where does a base of 12 actually land?
'' ============================================================================
''  A 4-pin mode's D[19:16] template is %p00a: ONE pin bit at D[19], and D[18:17]
''  fixed zero.  Read with the Silicon Doc's rule -- D[22:20] is the group in
''  8-pin increments, D[19:17] resolves within it -- base 12 means D[22:20]=%001
''  (group base 8) and D[19]=1 (+4), so P12..P15.  The KB's old table said
''  something else.  This settles it without a jumper: give P8..P11 one pattern,
''  P12..P15 another, capture at base 12, and see which pattern comes back.
PRI arm_B_unaligned_base() | b, odd, wrote, late

  debug("-- Arm B: which four pins does base 12 read? --")

  drive_pattern(BASE,     %0011)         ' P8,P9 high  -> byte $33 if read here
  drive_pattern(ALT_BASE, %1100)         ' P14,P15 high -> byte $CC if read there
  wrote, late := capture(CAP_ALT, NCO_1_4, 0)
  b, odd := uniform_byte()
  release_all()

  debug("B base 12   : bytes ", udec_(wrote), " first ", uhex_byte_(b), " unlike ", udec_(odd))
  if wrote == 0
    debug("B INCONCLUSIVE -- sentinel intact; nothing was captured.")
    return
  if b == BYTE_TOP2
    debug("B -> P12..P15, as predicted. D[19] is pin bit 2 and the base pin is")
    debug("   simply pin<<17. Confirms the F-361 correction to pin-selection.yaml.")
  elseif b == BYTE_LOW2
    debug("B -> P8..P11. THE PREDICTION IS WRONG. D[19:17] is behaving as a dense")
    debug("   slot selector, the old KB table was right, and F-361 must be")
    debug("   REVERSED before anything built on it ships.")
  else
    debug("B -> neither pattern. Record the byte; do not force a story.")
    dump_head(string("B"))


'' ============================================================================
''  ARM C -- the same contrast with NO STREAMER INVOLVED
'' ============================================================================
''  If the IN rule is real, it is visible to a plain cog read: while the smart pin
''  is toggling its pin, pinread() of THAT pin returns the smart pin's completion
''  flag (low for the whole burst) while pinread() of the routed neighbour returns
''  the pin's level (toggling).  Establishing that here means arm D's result
''  cannot be blamed on the capture path, and vice versa -- the simplest layer
''  answers first.
''  COUNT CHANGES, NOT LEVELS.  Over a 20 ms window containing a 10 ms burst,
''  BOTH signals spend about half the window high -- the flag because it rises at
''  burst end and stays up, the waveform because it is a square wave.  Level
''  counts therefore look ALIKE for the two things this arm exists to separate.
''  The number that separates them is how many TIMES each changed: the flag
''  changes once, the waveform changes once per half period.
PRI arm_C_cog_level_contrast() | i, bus_ch, mon_ch, bus_p, mon_p, v

  debug("-- Arm C: cog-level contrast, no streamer --")

  setup_stimulus_and_monitors(SLOW_PER)
  wypin(BUS, BURST_C)                    ' WYPIN(PinField, Data): start a 10 ms burst

  bus_p := pinread(BUS)
  mon_p := pinread(MON_R)
  repeat i from 0 to C_SAMPLES-1
    v := pinread(BUS)
    if v <> bus_p
      bus_ch++
      bus_p := v
    v := pinread(MON_R)
    if v <> mon_p
      mon_ch++
      mon_p := v
    waitus(C_INTERVAL)

  release_all()

  debug("   half period 300 us, sampled every 50 us, 12 ms burst in a 20 ms window")
  debug("C P8 (smart pin IN) changes : ", udec_(bus_ch), "  expect 1")
  debug("C P9 (routed -1)    changes : ", udec_(mon_ch), "  expect about 40")

  if mon_ch >= 20 and bus_ch <= 2
    debug("C AS PREDICTED -- the routed neighbour tracked the wire while the smart")
    debug("   pin's own IN changed once. IN on that pin is the completion flag.")
  elseif mon_ch >= 20 and bus_ch >= 20
    debug("C REFUTED at cog level -- P8's IN tracked the waveform too. The")
    debug("   IN-repurposing rule does not hold for this mode. Arm D will agree;")
    debug("   the KB blocks that rest on it must be regraded.")
  elseif mon_ch < 4
    debug("C STIMULUS DID NOT RUN -- P9 never changed. The TRANSITION smart pin is")
    debug("   not toggling: check P_OE, that DIR went high, and that WYPIN came")
    debug("   AFTER DIRH. Arm D's flat lanes would mean the same thing, and would")
    debug("   NOT be evidence about the IN rule.")
  else
    debug("C UNEXPECTED. Record both counts; do not force a story.")


'' ============================================================================
''  ARM D -- THE HEADLINE. Both signals, one buffer, one instant.
'' ============================================================================
PRI arm_D_streamer_contrast() | wrote, late, t0, o0, e0, z0, t1, o1, e1, z1, t2, o2, e2, z2, t3, o3, e3, z3

  debug("-- Arm D: smart-pin lane vs neighbour lane, in ONE capture --")

  setup_stimulus_and_monitors(FAST_PER)
  wrote, late := capture(CAP_BASE, NCO_1_1, BURST_D)
  release_all()

  if wrote == 0
    debug("D INCONCLUSIVE -- sentinel intact; the streamer never wrote.")
    return
  if late
    debug("D NOTE -- the capture hit its budget without XFI. Read on, but the")
    debug("   transfer may have been cut short; check the raw dump below.")

  t0, o0, e0, z0 := lane_stats(0)        ' P8  BUS   -- expect the FLAG
  t1, o1, e1, z1 := lane_stats(1)        ' P9  MON_R -- expect the WAVEFORM
  t2, o2, e2, z2 := lane_stats(2)        ' P10 MON_F -- waveform, filtered path
  t3, o3, e3, z3 := lane_stats(3)        ' P11 NULL  -- expect nothing at all

  debug("   1024 samples, one per clock. edges = 0<->1 changes in that lane.")
  debug("   each row: edges, highs, index of first edge")
  debug("D P8  BUS  smartpin : ", udec_(t0), " ", udec_(o0), " ", udec_(e0))
  debug("D P9  MON_R route-1 : ", udec_(t1), " ", udec_(o1), " ", udec_(e1))
  debug("D P10 MON_F filt0   : ", udec_(t2), " ", udec_(o2), " ", udec_(e2))
  debug("D P11 NULL  control : ", udec_(t3), " ", udec_(o3), " ", udec_(e3))
  dump_head(string("D"))

  '' The in-buffer control first. If the lane that must be silent is not, the
  '' lane mapping in this buffer is wrong and no other lane here means anything.
  if t3 <> 0 or o3 <> 0
    debug("D VOID -- the NULL lane (P11, driven low) carries data. The lane")
    debug("   mapping is wrong for THIS capture even though arm A passed.")
    debug("   Nothing else in arm D is interpretable.")
    return

  if t1 == 0
    debug("D STIMULUS DEAD -- the neighbour lane is flat, so nothing was toggling.")
    debug("   Read arm C: if C also saw no toggling, fix the smart pin, not the")
    debug("   capture. This is NOT evidence about the IN rule either way.")
    return

  if t0 <= 1 and t1 >= 8
    debug("D CONFIRMED -- the smart pin's lane carries at most ONE edge while its")
    debug("   neighbour's lane carries the waveform. Capturing a pin that is")
    debug("   running a smart-pin mode yields the IN handshake, not the traffic;")
    debug("   capturing a neighbour routed from it yields the traffic.")
    debug("   -> smart_pins.yaml in_signal_semantics and streamer/pin-capture.yaml")
    debug("      are grounded. Replicate as an EF entry.")
  elseif t0 >= 8
    debug("D REFUTED -- the smart pin's lane carries the WAVEFORM too. IN is NOT")
    debug("   repurposed by this smart-pin mode. STOP: smart_pins.yaml")
    debug("   in_signal_semantics and streamer/pin-capture.yaml both rest on the")
    debug("   opposite claim and must be regraded before anything ships.")
  else
    debug("D PARTIAL -- edge counts fall between the two predictions. Record them")
    debug("   and the raw bytes above; do not round toward either story.")

  if t2 > 0 and e2 >= e1
    debug("D SKEW: filtered lane's first edge is later by samples: ", udec_(e2 - e1))
    debug("   One sample = one clock = 5 ns here. That number is MEASURED;")
    debug("   no source we hold states a filter latency to compare it against.")
  elseif t2 > 0
    debug("D SKEW: filtered lane's first edge is EARLIER than unfiltered. That")
    debug("   cannot be a filter delay -- record it, do not explain it away.")
  else
    debug("D SKEW: not measurable -- the filtered lane carried no edge.")


'' ============================================================================
''  ARM E -- how fast can this be sustained? MEASURED, not asserted.
'' ============================================================================
''  The stimulus toggles every FAST_PER clocks.  At a sample every N clocks the
''  recovered half-cycle must be exactly FAST_PER/N samples wide.  If the path
''  cannot sustain a rate, the recovered spacing stops matching -- dropped samples
''  shrink it, a stalled streamer stretches it.  The fastest ratio that still
''  matches IS the ceiling, and nothing here predicts what that will be.
PRI arm_E_rate_ladder()
  debug("-- Arm E: sustained rate. predicted vs measured half-cycle width --")
  debug("   stimulus toggles every ", udec_(FAST_PER), " clocks throughout.")
  rate_leg(string("1:16"), NCO_1_16, FAST_PER / 16)
  rate_leg(string("1:8 "), NCO_1_8,  FAST_PER / 8)
  rate_leg(string("1:4 "), NCO_1_4,  FAST_PER / 4)
  rate_leg(string("1:2 "), NCO_1_2,  FAST_PER / 2)
  rate_leg(string("1:1 "), NCO_1_1,  FAST_PER)
  debug("   The fastest row whose measured width equals its predicted width is")
  debug("   the sustained ceiling for a 4-pin capture on this board. Report the")
  debug("   ROW, not a rounded number.")


PRI rate_leg(label, xfrq, want_width) | wrote, late, edges, highs, first, last, width

  setup_stimulus_and_monitors(FAST_PER)
  wrote, late := capture(CAP_BASE, xfrq, BURST_E)
  release_all()

  if wrote == 0
    debug("E ", zstr_(label), " : sentinel intact -- nothing captured.")
    return
  if late
    debug("E ", zstr_(label), " : hit the budget without XFI -- row is suspect.")

  edges, highs, first, last := lane_stats(1)   ' the unfiltered neighbour lane
  if edges < 2
    debug("E ", zstr_(label), " : ", udec_(edges), " edge(s) -- cannot measure a width.")
    return

  '' Mean samples per half-cycle, measured BETWEEN the first and last edge. Using
  '' the whole buffer instead would fold in the lead-in before the first edge and
  '' the tail after the last, and bias every row low by a couple of samples --
  '' enough to make a correct row look like a failed one.
  width := (last - first) / (edges - 1)
  debug("E ", zstr_(label), " bytes ", udec_(wrote), " edges ", udec_(edges))
  debug("        width ", udec_(width), " want ", udec_(want_width), " highs ", udec_(highs))


'' ============================================================================
''  Stimulus and monitors
'' ============================================================================
''  Order matters and is the project's universal one: reset (DIRL) -> configure
''  (WRPIN/WXPIN) -> enable (DIRH) -> operate (WYPIN).  WYPIN comes AFTER DIRH;
''  a WYPIN issued before enable is lost in the trigger modes (EF-011), and
''  TRANSITION is one of them.
PRI setup_stimulus_and_monitors(period)
  release_all()

  '' P8: TRANSITION output. P_OE is required or the smart pin does not drive the
  '' pin at all, and then every monitor lane reads a pin nobody is driving.
  pinclear(BUS)
  wrpin(BUS, P_TRANSITION | P_OE)
  wxpin(BUS, period)                     ' X[15:0] = base period in clocks
  pinlow(BUS)                            ' DIR high, OUT low: enable the smart
                                         ' pin. TRANSITION mode overrides OUT
                                         ' anyway, and "during reset the output
                                         ' is low", so this is the state the
                                         ' mode itself starts from. WYPIN, which
                                         ' actually starts it, comes AFTER this.

  '' P9: smart pin mode OFF (%SSSSS = %00000) with the A input selected from
  '' relative -1. THAT is the whole mechanism: with the mode field zero, "the
  '' resultant 'A' will drive the IN signal", and 'A' is P8's read state.
  '' DIR stays LOW -- a monitor must not drive the net it is watching.
  pinclear(MON_R)
  wrpin(MON_R, P_MINUS1_A)

  '' P10: the same signal two pins away, through filt0, so arm D can measure any
  '' added latency as a lane-to-lane skew.
  pinclear(MON_F)
  wrpin(MON_F, P_MINUS2_A | P_FILT0_AB)

  '' P11: no routing, no smart pin, driven low. The lane that must stay silent.
  pinclear(NULLPIN)
  pinlow(NULLPIN)

  waitms(1)


PRI drive_static(nib)
  drive_pattern(BASE, nib)


'' Drive four consecutive pins to the levels in the low four bits of `nib`,
'' bit 0 -> first pin. Plain pins: no smart mode, no routing, DIR high.
PRI drive_pattern(first_pin, nib) | i
  repeat i from 0 to 3
    pinclear(first_pin + i)
    if nib & (1 << i)
      pinhigh(first_pin + i)
    else
      pinlow(first_pin + i)
  waitms(1)


PRI release_all()
  org
    xstop
  end
  pinclear(GUARD_LO addpins GUARD_N-1)


'' ============================================================================
''  The capture itself
'' ============================================================================
''  WRFAST establishes the hub FIFO for writing -- "For WRFAST modes, it is
''  necessary to do a WRFAST sometime beforehand" -- with D = block count 0
''  (no wrapping, sequence the whole map) and S = the hub start address.
''  XINIT then runs for D[15:0] NCO rollovers and stops itself.
''
''  The wait is BOUNDED. WAITXFI would be the natural instruction and it is what
''  Parallax's own example uses, but an unbounded WAITXFI on a streamer that
''  never starts hangs the session with no diagnostic -- which cost this campaign
''  a bench slot in round 1. POLLXFI inside a budgeted loop gives the same answer
''  and a named failure instead of a dead terminal.
''
''  A LEFTOVER FLAG CANNOT END THE WAIT EARLY, and that is not an assumption: the
''  Silicon Doc says the POLLXFI/WAITXFI event flag is "Cleared on
''  XINIT/XZERO/XCONT". The XINIT below therefore clears whatever the previous
''  capture left set, so the first POLLXFI that returns C is reporting THIS
''  transfer and not the last one.
''
''  The trailing WAITX is the FIFO drain. The Silicon Doc's own guidance for a
''  cog that has been writing via WRFAST is a WAITX before it stops; 10,000
''  clocks is 500x that, which costs 50 us and removes the question.
PRI capture(mode, xfrq, ycnt) : written, late | addr, budget, t0, t1

  bytefill(@bufl, SENTINEL, BUF_BYTES)
  addr   := @bufl
  budget := CAP_BUDGET

  org
    setxfrq xfrq
    wrfast  #0, addr
    getct   t0
    xinit   mode, #0
    wypin   ycnt, #BUS          ' starts the burst; harmless when ycnt = 0
.wait
    getct   t1
    sub     t1, t0
    cmp     t1, budget      wc
    if_nc   mov     late, #1        ' streamer never signalled XFI in budget
    if_nc   jmp     #.done
    pollxfi                 wc
    if_nc   jmp     #.wait
.done
    waitx   ##10_000
    xstop
  end

  written := bytes_written()


'' How many bytes of the buffer are no longer the sentinel. This separates
'' "captured zeros" from "captured nothing" -- the distinction EF-059's rig was
'' built around, and the one a buffer of $00 cannot make on its own.
PRI bytes_written() : n | i
  repeat i from 0 to BUF_BYTES-1
    if byte[@bufl][i] <> SENTINEL
      n++


'' The buffer's first byte, and how many bytes differ from it. Arms A and B drive
'' STATIC levels, so every captured byte must be identical; `odd` counts the ones
'' that are not, and a non-zero count is itself the finding.
'' An earlier draft folded this into a -1 return, which printed as $FF and was
'' indistinguishable from a legitimate all-high capture. Report both numbers.
PRI uniform_byte() : v, odd | i
  v := byte[@bufl][0]
  repeat i from 1 to BUF_BYTES-1
    if byte[@bufl][i] <> v
      odd++


'' Per-lane statistics over all SAMPLES sample sets.
''   lane 0 = pin BASE+0 ... lane 3 = pin BASE+3   (established by arm A)
''   trans  = number of 0<->1 changes
''   highs  = number of samples reading 1
''   first  = index of the first change, or SAMPLES if the lane never changed
''   last   = index of the last change, or SAMPLES if the lane never changed
PRI lane_stats(lane) : trans, highs, first, last | i, v, prev
  first := SAMPLES
  last  := SAMPLES
  prev  := sample_bit(0, lane)
  if prev
    highs := 1
  repeat i from 1 to SAMPLES-1
    v := sample_bit(i, lane)
    if v
      highs++
    if v <> prev
      trans++
      if first == SAMPLES
        first := i
      last := i
      prev := v


'' One 4-bit sample set per NCO rollover, two per byte, earlier sample in the low
'' nibble (bits bottom-first, X_ALT_OFF). Bit 0 of the sample is the base pin.
PRI sample_bit(n, lane) : v | b
  b := byte[@bufl][n >> 1]
  if n & 1
    b >>= 4
  v := (b >> lane) & 1


PRI dump_head(label) | i
  debug("   raw ", zstr_(label), " first 16 bytes:")
  repeat i from 0 to 15
    debug("      ", udec_(i), ": ", uhex_byte_(byte[@bufl][i]))
